  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A strong cold front is expected to cross \nthrough the region today, resulting in widespread showers and \nthunderstorms. Flash flooding, damaging wind gusts and tornadoes\nare possible today.\n\nEarly afternoon update... updated to tweak pops and QPF upward\nand expand flood watch to the remainder of the northern and\nwestern sections of the forecast area. Remainder below is\nunchanged. \n\nTo the south of a closed upper low over eastern Canada, broad \ntroughing will be in place across much of the Northeast and Mid-\nAtlantic. An embedded mid-level shortwave will pass through the\nbase of the trough today. At the surface, a warm front will \nmove into portions of the area tonight as a cold front will \nbegin to approach the area on Thursday.\n\nWith guidance coming into better agreement with the timing and \nplacement of frontal boundaries in our area, there has been an \nuptick in confidence in what the evolution of what should\ntranspire later today. Multiple rounds of widespread showers \nand storms are expected. With the cold front approaching from \nthe west, and a warm front expected to set-up across the \nsouthern half of the area across the Delmarva and into southern \nNew Jersey, we will have to watch these areas for severe weather\nthis afternoon and evening. \n\nBulk shear values up to 30-40 kt support organized clusters \nwhere locally damaging wind gusts are possible. Some of the 00z \nCAMs have been suggesting some isolated discrete supercells \ndeveloping out ahead of the main batch of convection. Thinking \nthe models may have a good idea of how things will evolve today,\nwith scattered showers and thunderstorms starting out as \nindividual discrete cells in the early afternoon with a more \nlinear, multi-cell clusters taking over and pushing through by \nthe mid-afternoon and into the early evening.\n\nWith the warm front draped over portions of the area, \nthere will be a localized corridor of a tornado threat, \nespecially where any storm is able to latch onto the boundary. \nSPC has introduced a 5% tornado risk over SE PA, northern \nDelaware, and the southern half of New Jersey. Locally backed \nflow at the surface near/south of the warm front will enhance \nlow-level shear. In addition to increased low-level shear, we \nwill have a very moist airmass overhead and low LCLs - factors \nfavorable for tornadogenesis. Some question remains in how far \nnorth the warm sector gets, which will have implications for \nwhere the largest tornado threat is. For now though, the \nforecast currently has the area south of the PA Turnpike and \nI-195 as the main area to watch. This does include the Philly \nmetro area. Cannot rule out isolated large hail as well as \ntemperatures aloft are rather cool for this time of year. Cloud\ncover should increase at the morning goes on, which could be a\nlimiting factor in terms of instability buildup. The Slight \n(2/5) Risk remains for most of the area.\n\nThe other, and possibly more concerning threat may be the risk \nand potential for flash flooding across portions of the area. \nMost guidance continues to depict a very moisture rich airmass \nsurging northward today where PWAT values are expected to top \nout around 2.2-2.6 inches. Model forecast soundings also show \ntall, skinny CAPE profiles and a relatively deep warm cloud \nlayer. The one saving grace is that the overall shear vectors \nindicate that storms should be 'relatively' quick moving. \nHowever, any given storm will be capable of producing highly \nefficient rain rates, at times around 2 to 4 inches per hour. \nWith this being said, WPC has maintained a SLIGHT risk (Level \n2/4) for excessive rainfall today over most of the area. There\nwas talk of adding a Moderate Risk around the Philly metro, but\nhave held off for now. Added Morris County, NJ to the Flood\nWatch since there was a slight trend north with the highest QPF.\nOtherwise, no changes. The Flash Flood Watch remains in place\nfor most of New Jersey, southeastern PA, and northern Delaware. \n\nIn general, expecting anywhere between 1 to 2 inches of rain to\nfall, with localized higher amounts up to 3 to 5 inches.\nGuidance has remained relatively consistent all throughout\ntoday. We will have efficient rainfall producers where some\nareas could see multiple rounds, which is not a good\ncombination. This event will also be during the evening commute,\nwith travel disruptions likely. Have multiple ways to receive\nwarnings. \n\nThe severe and flooding rain threat should diminish overnight \ninto Friday morning.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Conditions look to remain unsettled through the\nweekend, with daily chances of showers and storms each day.\n\nMid to long range NWP and AI guidance suggests that the pattern\ncould become unsettled Friday night and into the weekend. \nThe cold front will move offshore tonight but stall just\noffshore. Multiple waves of low pressure will ride along the\nstalled front, resulting in unsettled weather through the\nweekend. The main periods to watch are early Saturday through\nSaturd",
